# CELL: `.or_else()` × Error-axis CROSS-TYPE × Result[int, Money] → Result[int, int]
# Round XXIII Track α (2026-08-01) — SBO class-fix on cross-type Result.or_else.
#
# Pre-fix bug: `src/ir/lowering/exprs/methods.rs::try_lower_option_result_combinator`
# had NO `or_else` arm in the `result_type = match method_name { … }` block at
# ~:3746; `or_else` fell through to `_ => recv_type`. When the closure returned
# `Result[T, E']` with `E' ≠ E`, `result_local` was born as `Result[T, E]`
# (recv_type, mis-sized). Both branch memcpys at :3985 and the merge memcpy at
# :4022 then read/wrote the wrong number of bytes → AddressSanitizer trip:
# `stack-buffer-overflow READ of size 80 at 0x… (24-byte src)` inside
# `__interceptor_memcpy` at the `via_param` merge. Both C and LLVM SBOd. RED-
# verified pre-fix (Round XXIII scout `/tmp/round_xxiii_trackAlpha_scout_1.md`
# `probe_sbo_exact.gg`).
#
# Fix (single arm extension, Core #1 write-site + Core #4 class-fix):
#   - `methods.rs:3779` — add `"or_else"` to the `"and_then" | "flat_map"` arm
#     so `result_type` is derived from `infer_closure_return_type(closure_op)`,
#     sizing `result_local` from the closure's DECLARED return.
#   - `methods.rs:3701-3715` — mirror `and_then`'s dual `expected_type` rule
#     for `or_else` (outer Option/Result annotation wins over recv-forced hint)
#     so annotated cross-type shapes coerce the closure body correctly.
#   - `typecheck.rs::unify_closure_ret_axis` (new shared helper) — enforces
#     `Result.or_else` Ok-unify (`T' == T`, allow `E' ≠ E` — that IS the
#     recovery axis), `Result.and_then` Err-unify (`E' == E`, allow `U ≠ T`),
#     and `Option.or_else` Some-unify (`T' == T`). 3-cell class, ratcheted
#     by `tests/lints.rs::unify_closure_ret_axis_class_enumeration`.
#
# POST-FIX GREEN: ASan-clean on both C and LLVM lanes. Recovery closure
# translates the heap-live Money error into an int summary.
#
# HEAP-FORCED Money (same pattern as `combinator_result_or_else_money_passthrough.gg`):
# a literal `Money(Vector[int]())` would drop `.len() == 0` and not stress the
# heap allocator — the SBO required a real heap buffer through the branch memcpy.
# The recovery function `.cents.len()` reads the Money's Vector length to prove
# the buffer stays live across the branch (no premature drop).

struct Money:
    Vector[int] cents

Vector[int] heap_cents(int n):
    Vector[int] c = Vector[int]()
    int i = 0
    while i < n:
        c.push(i)
        i = i + 1
    return c

Result[int, int] recover(Money m):
    return Ok(m.cents.len())

void via_param(Result[int, Money] p):
    # E-axis cross: recv Result[int, Money], closure returns Result[int, int].
    # Under Ok-unify T'==T (int==int) ✓; E'≠E (int≠Money) is the recovery axis.
    Result[int, int] r = p.or_else((Money m): recover(m))
    if r is Ok(v):
        print(v)

void main():
    via_param(Error(Money(heap_cents(5))))
